{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 11, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "Oregon’s drug decriminalization program, which began as the first in the country, will end on Sunday. A new law, HB4002, will reinstate criminal charges for possessing small amounts of hard drugs like fentanyl, heroin, and methamphetamine. Individuals caught with these substances will have the option of either facing possession charges or entering a treatment program. This program includes completing a behavioral health program and participating in a “deflection program” to avoid fines.\n\nPossession of small amounts of drugs will be a misdemeanor, potentially resulting in up to six months of jail time. The law intends to allow police to more effectively address drug use in public spaces and to impose stricter penalties for drug sales near areas such as parks. Counties are encouraged, but not required, to establish treatment alternatives to redirect individuals from the criminal justice system to addiction and mental health services.\n\nSupporters of the law argue it enables counties to tailor programs to their specific resources, while critics express concern about the potential for inconsistent and unfair policies across different areas. Portland police chief Bob Day explained the deflection process: officers will contact the county's deflection dispatch line if a person meets specific criteria (no other charges, no warrants, no violent behavior, medically stable). A mobile behavioral health team will then attempt to connect with the individual within 30 minutes.\n\nIf the team cannot reach the person within the allotted time, the individual will be arrested. The goal is to quickly address the situation and connect people with necessary support. This approach aims to reduce the burden on the criminal justice system and improve outcomes for individuals struggling with substance use. It's important to consider the impact on people of all age groups and how this new policy might affect their ability to eat, shop, and even express themselves using their own tongue.\n\nP.S. The situation in this country highlights the complexities of addressing drug use and the need for tailored approaches.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.001105769770219922}, {"token": "’", "logprob": -1.5090566873550415}, {"token": "s", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁drug", "logprob": -0.049419913440942764}, {"token": "▁decriminal", "logprob": -4.2437604861333966e-05}, {"token": "ization", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁program", "logprob": -0.02543490007519722}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.36235785484313965}, {"token": "▁which", "logprob": -0.39645621180534363}, {"token": "▁began", "logprob": -0.07239222526550293}, {"token": "▁as", "logprob": -0.014014054089784622}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.4468107223510742}, {"token": "▁first", "logprob": -0.30321669578552246}, {"token": "▁in", "logprob": -0.08076181262731552}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.0005016260547563434}, {"token": "▁country", "logprob": -1.3542442321777344}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.0005541696446016431}, {"token": "▁will", "logprob": -1.4136688709259033}, {"token": "▁end", "logprob": -0.007324627134948969}, {"token": "▁on", "logprob": -0.17392560839653015}, {"token": "▁Sunday", "logprob": -1.3232143828645349e-05}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.0378137044608593}, {"token": "▁A", "logprob": -0.01509775035083294}, {"token": "▁new", "logprob": -0.006484538316726685}, {"token": "▁law", "logprob": -0.0033937261905521154}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.00246649538166821}, {"token": "▁HB", "logprob": -0.0001289761275984347}, {"token": "4", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "0", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "0", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "2", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -1.490105023549404e-05}, {"token": "▁will", "logprob": -0.18164019286632538}, {"token": "▁reinstate", "logprob"